Blow-by-blow documentation of what STRIP.TEC does internally

Copyright 2004-2014 CLA Systems
ClaSystems@gmail.com

Last edit: 06-Apr-2014 cjl

The TECO macro STRIP.TEC is executed by the following batch command:

TECOC MUNG STRIP.TEC,%PTO%

within prevailing batch files.

This document will explain what this accomplishes and in most instances exactly how.

Note:  Except where otherwise indicated, $ refers to the escape [<ESC>] character
[ASCII code 27 decimal, 32 octal], which has no defined printing character; you see
a "$" character as a display substitution when a TECO macro command containing <ESC>
characters is typed out on the TECO command line; this includes displaying the
contents of the edit buffer.  This is why it is difficult to edit a TECO macro other
than within TECO itself.  The <ESC> character is used as a terminator of a string
or command in most instances; a macro must contain <ESC> characters by nature.
Making matters more complicated, TECO macros are known to dynamically create other
TECO macros to be executed from the main TECO macro program.  As such, these created
TECO macros likely have to have <ESC> characters within their contests.
Fortunately, TECO allows direct insertion of characters by code; thus, the sequence
27I$ is often seen.  The "I" [Insert] command, when passed a numerical argument [and
terminated by an <ESC> character], creates a single character in the edit buffer
with the specified code; there are no reserved values.  The numerical form of the
"I" command requires a trailing <ESC> character to distinguish it from the text form
of the "I" command which [potentialy] allows multiple insertions of text strings.
[Note: Specifics of the text form of the "I" command could be version-dependent;
STRIP.TEC does not depend on these command extensions.]  The text form of the "I"
command inserts an indefinitely long text string into the edit buffer; the numerical
form of the "I" command inserts one character; the character code is the numerical
argument used in the command.  [Note: Various TECO commands have a similar duality
of functionality determined by exact usage.]

The description of the entire STRIP.TEC follows.  Afterwards, each section is
explained as to what it does.  Please note that line breaks [CR/LF] are meaningless
within TECO except when those characters would be part of a string passed to the
text form of an "I" [Insert] command. Thus, within this document, it is merely a
line break added for readability.  [The actual STRIP.TEC file may not have identical
line break characters within.]

STRIP.TEC Contents

                    ╔═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╗
                    ║                                                         ║
                    ║  EZ#128EZ$HXQHKGQ0JIEB$ZJ27I$HXBHKMB                    ║
                    ║  Y0J<N^X$;-1A&127U1-D                                   ║
                    ║  Q1-26"NONOTEND$'."EONOCHARS$'0,.P$0,.K$!NOCHARS!EFEX$  ║
                    ║  !NOTEND!Q1"EOIGNORE$'Q1-127"EOIGNORE$'                 ║
                    ║  Q1-12"NONOTFF$'.USQH"EOIGNORE$'0A+1"EOIGNORE$'         ║
                    ║  !LOOP1!0A&127"NOLFTEST$'DOLOOP1$                       ║
                    ║  !LFTEST!0A&127-10"NONOTFF$'C$                          ║
                    ║  !LOOP2!0A&127"NOENDTEST$'DOLOOP2$                      ║
                    ║  !ENDTEST!0A+1"NONOTEOF$'QS"EONOCH$'0,QSP$0,QSK$        ║
                    ║  !NOCH!EFEX$!NOTEOF!QSJOPUTIN$                          ║
                    ║  !NOTFF!Q1-10"NOPUTIN$'-1A-13"EOPUTIN$'13I$             ║
                    ║  !PUTIN!Q1I$Q1-13"NONOTCR$'0A&127-10"N10I$'             ║
                    ║  !NOTCR!%H!IGNORE!>EFEX$$                               ║
                    ║                                                         ║
                    ╚═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╝

Code fragment       Explanation

EZ#128EZ$           EZ is a version-dependent internal register of TECOC with
                    various control functions which can affect overall operation.
                    The value already contained in the EZ register is inclusively
                    .OR.ed with the constant 128 then written back to the EZ
                    register.  By forcing this specific bit to the on state, buffer
                    appendage limited by <FF> characters is inhibited.  Other TECO
                    implementations could handle this situation differently or not
                    at all; the logic of the STRIP.TEC macro depends on this
                    operational behavior.

HXQ                 The entire text in the edit buffer is copied into Q-register Q.
                    The referenced text is the passed complete pathname argument,
                    which is the expansion of the %PTO% environment variable such
                    as:

                    %PDP-8_DRIVE%\PDP-8\SIMH\PDP-8\SIMH_Working_Files\PTPFILE.TXT

                    which in turn contains an environment variable; this reduces to
                    something of the form:

                    J:\PDP-8\SIMH\PDP-8\SIMH_Working_Files\PTPFILE.TXT

                    by the time the text is placed into the edit buffer
                    automatically by invoking TECOC in this manner.  [Note: The PTO
                    environment variable is derived from the %1 argument to the
                    overall batch file that initiated the TECOC command.
                    Command-line arguments to invoke TECO are extremely
                    system-dependent; this method is valid for MS-DOS and WINDOWS
                    TECOC.]

HK                  Kills the passed edit buffer, since it is now saved in
                    Q-register Q.

GQ                  Brings the passed string back into the edit buffer.  [Note: This
                    method is used to allow additional file commands perhaps needed
                    in a future version of the program.]

0JIEB$              Jump to the beginning of the buffer and insert "EB" there.  "EB"
                    is the TECO command to update an existing file; this is a
                    command being formed and not yet ready to be executed as a TECO
                    macro.

ZJ27I$              A trailing <ESC> character is required to complete the macro
                    command when it is executed.  The buffer pointer is adjusted to
                    point to the end of the buffer and <ESC> is placed into the
                    buffer to complete the command.


HXB                 The entire edit buffer contents, which is something of the form:

                    EBJ:\PDP-8\SIMH\PDP-8\SIMH_Working_Files\PTPFILE.TXT$

                    is placed into Q-register B.

HK$                 Kills the edit buffer, since it is now saved in Q-register B.
                    Note: TECO macros occasionally contain extraneous <ESC>
                    characters for formatting purposes only.  If the context doesn't
                    require an <ESC> character, it is ignored.  This is somewhat
                    analogous to when programmers of more typical mathematically
                    oriented languages place superfluous sets of parentheses around
                    expressions even when precedence rules might not require this.
                    Occasionally, this is also done to make the overall editing
                    process of the TECO macro slightly easier.  [Note: TECO and
                    several other languages do not support any form of precedence
                    rules; mathematical expressions are evaluated strictly left to
                    right.]

MB$                 Q-register B's string contents is executed as a TECO Macro.
                    This setups up the passed file to be updated.

Y                   The "Yank" command reads in some portion of the file into the
                    editing buffer.  Since <FF> checking is inhibited, the buffer
                    size alone determines the size of the portion loaded.  TECO
                    implementations vary as to buffer size; a number of additional
                    locations are reserved in case the buffer grows due to the
                    various macro and other operations.  In the case of the
                    STRIP.TEC program, the file size will change very little
                    depending on the exact nature of what is being stripped out; a
                    few operations might actually add as many as one or two
                    characters for each occurrence of a special case; however, this
                    would occur very infrequently.  Most characters are merely
                    processed to remove undesirable high-order bits; as such, the
                    overall character count will hardly change.

0J                  Reset the buffer pointer to the beginning of the edit buffer.
                    Most TECO implementations will do this automatically after a
                    Yank operation.  TECO programmers often include this to clarify
                    the scope of an iteration loop that follows.

<                   Because of the leading "<" character, what follows up to a
                    matching ">" character is executed in an infinite loop; various
                    methods exist to break out of an infinite loop, such as a
                    failing search operation.  This is the primary operational loop
                    of most of the program.

N^X$;               A Nonstop search is performed.  All characters processed will be
                    forced into the output buffer and [eventually] written out.  In
                    this case the search is a special-case operation; control-X
                    means the search argument is any single character of any value.
                    The only way this can fail is if the end of file is reached.
                    When any form of search fails inside of iteration brackets when
                    formed with a trailing ";" character, no error occurs; the
                    iteration loop ends and execution continues past the ">"
                    character matching the "<" character in effect when the failing
                    search was attempted.  Assuming the search succeeded, the buffer
                    pointer is now just past the matching text character.  Should a
                    character be inserted, the buffer pointer will be placed just
                    after the newly inserted character.  [Note: This form of
                    insertion is only done for certain special cases.]

-1A&127U1-D         When the "A" command is used with a leading numerical argument,
                    the argument is used as an offset from the buffer pointer to
                    locate a particular character in the buffer.  In this case, the
                    numeric value of the previous character is returned [which is
                    the character that matched the search as described above].  This
                    value is then masked via a logical .and. operation against the
                    mask constant 127 which preserves the low-order seven bits and
                    strips the high-order bit.  This corrected value is then stored
                    in Q-register 1; the referenced character is then removed from
                    the buffer.  [Later, the corrected character value will be
                    inserted into the buffer unless the character requires special
                    handling.]

Q1-26"NONOTEND$'    If the character is not control-Z [the expression evaluates to
                    other than zero; 26 is the character code for control-Z] then
                    gotO the portion of the program labeled !NOTEND!.  Otherwise,
                    execution continues inline to finalize the file.  Note: The
                    underlying operating system may have alternate conventions
                    regarding the end of file state from the operating system that
                    created the file; this is why end of file detection is also
                    carried out elsewhere in the program.  [Note: ASCII text files
                    created by/for OS/8 or the P?S/8 SHELL conform to this
                    convention.  Extraneous characters may exist in the file past
                    the control-Z; if present, they are to be ignored.  In extremely
                    rare situations, defective OS/8 utilities may create files that
                    end on the last character of the file with an active character;
                    control-Z might not be present.  This situation is handled
                    properly by this program; however, it may be necessary to remedy
                    this situation within OS/8 to add a proper sentinel control-Z
                    character.  MS-DOS and Windows do not support this convention
                    internally; exact byte counts are used to account for file
                    length.  P?S/8 conversion programs to/from OS/8 have safeguards
                    against this problem; OS/8 PIP operating in ASCII text mode
                    should prevent this problem; Kermit-12 for OS/8 implements
                    internal defensive measures to prevent this problem.]

."EONOCHARS$'       If the edit buffer pointer is zero, there were no characters in
                    the buffer when the control-Z character was detected.  This is
                    not a likely situation; however, this is part of the
                    belts-and-suspenders approach taken with this program.  Should
                    this be true, gotO the portion of the program labeled !NOCHARS!
                    to avoid the immediately following operations.

0,.P$0,.K$          All characters in the edit buffer are outPut [aka "Punched"];
                    then those characters are deleted from the edit buffer.  These
                    operations were skipped if the edit buffer was empty.  [The
                    label !NOCHARS! follows.]

!NOCHARS!EFEX$      The output file is closed using the "EF" [End File] command;
                    TECO then EXits to the operating system.

!NOTEND!            The rest of the program processes the latest [stripped]
                    character checking for various special cases.  [Note: All
                    operations are within the iteration brackets "<" and ">" and
                    will be performed repeatedly; each iteration starts with the
                    Non-stop search for any character [N^X$;] until the search
                    attempt fails.  All processing for any iteration ends at the
                    label !IGNORE!.]

Q1"EOIGNORE$'       If the latest character is zero [<NUL> character] gotO the
                    portion of the program labeled !IGNORE! which is the very end of
                    the main section of the program running in iteration.  Thus, all
                    <NUL> characters are removed.  Surprisingly, many OS/8 text
                    files processed through the PTP: handler contain large
                    quantities of <NUL> characters in large groups which must be
                    removed; certain special cases are also known to exist and will
                    be handled elsewhere.

Q1-127"EOIGNORE$'   If the latest character is a <RO> it is removed.  Various files
                    are known to contain extraneous <RO> characters for a variety of
                    reasons.

Q1-12"NONOTFF$'     If the latest character is not a <FF> [character code 12] gotO
                    the portion of the program labeled !NOTFF!.  This is necessary
                    because there are many anomalies in various files when a <FF>
                    character is issued; it is theorized that many of these problems
                    stem from an incomplete understanding of how to properly handle
                    logical pages intended for printing from various processing
                    utilities.  The source file convention for most PDP-8 files
                    separates pages by a single <FF> character; unfortunately,
                    various anomalies must be potentially dealt with when a <FF>
                    character is detected in the file.

.US                 The current edit buffer pointer is saved into Q-register S.
                    Certain operations are performed on characters past where the
                    latest <FF> was located; after these operations complete, the
                    buffer pointer can be restored.

QH"EOIGNORE$'       As a special concession to output files created by PAL8 and
                    related OS/8 utilities, if the first character in the file is a
                    <FF> character [after first removing all <NUL> and <RO>
                    characters], it will be removed.  Additional <NUL> or <RO>
                    characters may be in the file; they will be removed by other
                    routines as necessary.

                    [Note: P?S/8 PAL and related utilities from the circa 1978
                    release of P?S/8 do not conform to this convention; the first
                    <FF> and any prior <CR> and <LF> characters must be removed by
                    external means if desired.

                    Future versions of this program may handle this problem by
                    assuming that removal of a <FF> character not quite at the
                    beginning of a file is considered acceptable; extremely
                    pragmatic coding may be required to avoid data loss in certain
                    cases.  As of this writing, it is believed the exact sequence in
                    P?S/8 PAL LPT:-oriented output [after all <NUL> and <RO>
                    characters are removed] is CR LF CR LF FF; additional logic may
                    be added to expressly handle this special case.]

                    Note: This program depends on the known behavior of TECOC for
                    MS-DOS and Windows to initialize numeric Q-register storage to
                    zero.  This behavior is likely implementation-specific.  If the
                    TECO implementation supports errors on unitialized variables,
                    the program should be modifed to initialize variables such as
                    this using commands such as OUH.  [The numeric argument to the
                    "U" command is stored into the specified Q-register.]

                    Q-register H is incremented when any non-ignored character is
                    processed; as such, this operation only affects the initial <FF>
                    character.

0A+1"EOIGNORE$'     A feature of this program is to remove trailing <FF> characters
                    where possible; files are handled differently in the host
                    operating environment; print-oriented utilities process pages
                    based on other conventions which avoid extraneous blank pages.

                    TECO implements a safeguard at the end of file.  Attempts to
                    access additional characters will result in an error abort.  To
                    avoid this, if the code returned for the next character is -1,
                    the end of file has been reached.  GotO the portion of the
                    program labeled !IGNORE! to avoid processing this trailing <FF>
                    character; the next iteration will cause the search to fail and
                    break out of the iteration loop for final file processing.

                    [Note: If a file contains <NUL> or <RO> characters after the
                    last <FF> character, the condition checked for will not exist;
                    if this occurrs, it is likely the problem can be remedied by
                    running the program a second time on the stripped file; the net
                    result will be processed properly to remove the trailing <FF>
                    character.]

!LOOP1!             In certain OS/8 files, sequences of up to nine <NUL> characters
                    followed by a <LF> character are placed after every <FF>
                    character which must be removed.  The program iterates back to
                    !LOOP1! until all <NUL> characters after the <FF> character are
                    removed.

0A&127"NOLFTEST$'   If the next character after a <FF> character is not a <NUL>
                    character, gotO the portion of the program labeled !LFTEST!.

DOLOOP1$            If the next character after a <FF> character is a <NUL>
                    character, delete the character; gotO the portion of the program
                    labeled !LOOP1! to test further.

!LFTEST!            After all <NUL> characters after a <FF> character are removed,
                    the next character is likely a <LF> character.  Test for this
                    now.

0A&127-10"NONOTFF$' If the character after all <NUL> characters after a <FF>
                    character is not a <LF> character, gotO the portion of the
                    program labeled !NOTFF! to be processed as something other than
                    part of this specially checked sequence.

C$                  If the character after all <NUL> characters after a <FF>
                    character is a <LF> character, advance the buffer pointer to
                    preserve the <LF> character.

!LOOP2!             In certain files passed through the OS/8 PTP: handler, a long
                    sequence of trailing <NUL> characters may follow the last <FF>
                    character followed by nine <NUL> characters followed by <LF>.
                    All of these <NUL> characters will be removed.

0A&127"NOENDTEST$'  If the character after the <LF> character is not a <NUL>
                    character, gotO the portion of the program labeled !ENDTEST!.

DOLOOP2$            If the next character after the <LF> character is a <NUL>
                    character, delete this character; gotO the portion of the
                    program labeled !LOOP2! to test further.

!ENDTEST!           The extremely pragmatic sequence checked for so far includes the
                    requirement that the buffer pointer must be at the end-of-file.
                    If not, handle the <FF> character as a more typical case.

0A+1"NONOTEOF$'     If the next character after the last <NUL> character after the
                    <LF> character is not the end-of-file indicator, gotO the
                    portion of the program labeled !NOTEOF!.

QS"EONOCH$'         Q-register S contains the buffer pointer when the trailing <FF>
                    character was located before the now-validated trailing sequence
                    of additional characters.  If the contents of Q-register S is
                    zero, gotO the portion of the program labeled !NOCH!.

0,QSP$0,QSK$        All characters from the beginning of the buffer through the
                    position indicated in Q-register S will be outPut now.  All
                    characters from the beginning of the buffer through the position
                    indicated in Q-register S will be deleted.

!NOCH!EFEX$         After any existent characters in the buffer are processed [or
                    not if there weren't any] the file is closed with an "EF"
                    [End File] command.  The trailing <FF> and the entire sequence
                    of trailing characters was deleted from the file.  Teco then
                    EXits to the operating system.

!NOTEOF!QSJOPUTIN$  If the pragmatic sequence of characters expected after the
                    <FF> did not occur, the buffer pointer is restored to the
                    position saved in Q-Register J.  Control is transferred to the
                    portion of the program labeled !PUTIN! to handle the <FF> as a
                    routine character.

!NOTFF!             Control is transferred to this portion of the program if either
                    the latest character is not a <FF> character or the expected
                    <LF> character was not present.

Q1-10"NOPUTIN$'     If the latest character is not a <LF> character, gotO the
                    portion of the program labeled !PUTIN!.

-1A-13"EOPUTIN$'    If the latest character is a <LF> character, check if the
                    previous character is a <CR> character.  If so, gotO the portion
                    of the program labeled !PUTIN!.

13I$                If the latest character is a <LF> character and the previous
                    character is not a <CR> character, insert a <CR> character
                    before the <LF> character.  Many PDP-8 files fail to obey this
                    convention; the host operating system requires lines end with
                    <CR> <LF>.  Note: Files originating from unix systems may also
                    have the identical problem.

!PUTIN!Q1I$         Control is transferred to this portion of the program to insert
                    the stripped version of the original character.  Q-register I
                    contains the [possibly] corrected value which is inserted into
                    the edit buffer.

Q1-13"NONOTCR$'     If the latest character is not a <CR> character, gotO the
                    portion of the program labeled !NOTCR!.

0A&127-10"N10I$'    If the latest character is a <CR> character, test the next
                    character; if the next character is not a <LF> character, insert
                    a <LF> character.  Many PDP-8 files fail to obey this convention
                    although not nearly as often as failing to have a <CR> before a
                    <LF>.  This may be true if the file originates with various
                    Apple or other products.  Certain P?S/8 programs from the circa
                    1978 release have similar quirks; newer releases will fix these
                    problems [if not already fixed].

!NOTCR!%H           Q-register H is incremented to inhibit further special handling
                    of initial <FF> characters.  Other than the characters
                    associated with infrequent special handling, Q-register H will
                    generally contain the running character count of the file as it
                    is being processed.

!IGNORE!>EFEX$$     Any direct transfer of control to this point will ignore the
                    latest character.  All special-case routines that process
                    characters also come here to finish the iteration loop.

                    When the Non-stop search fails, control passes to the first
                    command outside of the iteration brackets.  The file is closed
                    with the "EF" [End File] command; TECO exits to the operating
                    system.

                    Since the entire macro string ends with two successive <ESC>
                    characters, this also ends the macro input into TECO when TECO
                    is initialized.  The exact process is version-dependent.  As
                    such, it is good practice to avoid the use of the textual
                    Q-registers X, Y, and Z, as in some TECO implementations, the
                    passing of macro arguments may resemble the following:

                    HXZMZ

                    In essence, the entire macro is treated as a text string in the
                    editing buffer as TECO starts; all characters are then placed
                    into Q-register Z [or X or Y depending on version] then the "MZ"
                    command is executed to start the Macro contents of Q-register Z.
                    In some versions of TECO, this is effected using a "hidden"
                    Q-register thus all of the 36 Q-registers are fully available.

[end-of-file]
